## Why This Is Asked
This question probes your understanding of end-to-end sampling strategies, cost-quality trade-offs, dynamic reconfiguration capabilities, and verification approaches in large-scale multi-region environments.

## Key Concepts
- Adaptive sampling with dynamic rate adjustment
- Tail-based sampling for critical path preservation
- Per-operation sampling policies
- Canary-based validation and rollback procedures
- Observability data budget management

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-adaptive sampling policy (high level)
function shouldSample(span) {
  // Sample aggressively for high-latency paths
  // Apply per-operation rate caps
  // Allow collector-tail decisions when budget tight
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure the impact on SLI accuracy during rollout?
- What metrics would you monitor to trigger automatic rollback?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical mastery of deduplication in real systems, combining transactional patterns, Kafka exactly-once semantics, and robust auditing.

## Key Concepts

- Exactly-once processing
- Transactional outbox pattern
- Kafka transactions (transactional.id)
- Idempotency keys and dedupe stores
- Compensating actions and audit trails

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: within a DB transaction
BEGIN;
UPDATE orders SET status='PAID' WHERE id = :orderId;
INSERT INTO outbox (order_id, event_type, payload, created_at) VALUES (:orderId, 'OrderPaid', :payload, NOW());
COMMIT;
```

```javascript
// Kafka publish path (conceptual)
producer.beginTransaction();
producer.send(outboxEvent);
producer.commitTransaction();
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate duplicates under failure modes (partition revocation, broker failure)?
- What metrics and logs would you instrument to verify end-to-end exactly-once behavior?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design scalable, tenant-aware incident response and degradation at scale. It tests how candidates balance automated safeguards with precise human oversight and how runbooks integrate metrics, backfills, and recovery verification.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-region, multi-tenant SLAs
- Degradation strategies and traffic shaping
- Cache strategy and data consistency during outages
- Canary testing, rollback criteria, post-incident validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo per-tenant degradation switch
function route(request){
  const t = request.tenant;
  if (t.degradation === 'high') return routeToCacheOnly();
  return routeToFullPath();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate this runbook in a synthetic outage? 
- Which dashboards and alerting thresholds would you use for tenants at risk?
